{"step_index":56,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:19Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:57:19+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:57:24+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Total Domination in Graphs\" \"Henning\" \"radius\" OR \"rad(G)\" proof\" returned the following summary:\nThe relationship between the **total domination number** ($\\gamma_t(G)$) and the **radius** ($\\text{rad}(G)$) of a graph is a recurring theme in the work of **Michael A. Henning** and his co-authors (notably in their research on distance domination and bounds for specific graph classes)[1][2][3][4].\n\n### Key Context and Relevant Proof Approaches\nIn the literature authored by Henning et al., the radius $\\text{rad}(G)$ is often used as a parameter to bound the total domination number, particularly for restricted classes of graphs (like planar graphs) or when considering distance-$k$ domination[1][3].\n\n*   **Distance Domination and Radius:** A common proof strategy for lower bounds involving the radius involves the construction of a spanning tree $T$[1][2]. Because adding edges to a graph cannot increase its radius, one can establish that $\\text{rad}(G) \\le \\text{rad}(T)$[1][2]. Since $T$ is a tree, the relationship between diameter, radius, and domination number is more easily calculated (e.g., using $\\text{diam}(T) \\ge 2\\text{rad}(T) - 1$)[2].\n*   **Planar Graphs:** Research by Dorfling, Goddard, and Henning[3][5] specifically explores how $\\text{diam}(G)$ and $\\text{rad}(G)$ influence $\\gamma_t(G)$. For instance, they showed that for planar graphs with diameter 3, the upper bound for the total domination number varies depending on whether the radius is 2 or 3[3].\n\n### Notable References\nIf you are looking for specific proofs, the following works by Henning are primary sources:\n\n1.  **\"Lower bounds on the distance domination number of a graph\"** (with F. Kenter, *Contributions to Discrete Mathematics*, 2017): This paper contains proofs relating distance $k$-domination to the radius of the graph[1][4]. It explicitly uses the property that $\\text{rad}(G) \\le \\text{rad}(T)$ for a spanning tree $T$[2].\n2.  **\"Total Domination in Graphs\"** (Springer Monographs in Mathematics, 2013, with A. Yeo): This is the definitive text on the subject[6][7]. It provides the foundational theorems and proofs for various bounds on $\\gamma_t(G)$, including those related to structural parameters like radius and diameter[8].\n3.  **\"A New Upper Bound on the Total Domination Number of a Graph\"** (with A. Yeo, 2007): Focuses on bounds for graphs with specified minimum/maximum degrees[9].\n\nTo find the specific proof you are looking for, it is recommended to consult **Chapter 2 or 3 of the Henning & Yeo monograph**[7][8], or the **2017 *Contributions to Discrete Mathematics* paper**[1][4], which contains the most direct technical application of the radius parameter in a proof context.\n\nSources:\n[1] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGNR40QE-QqZSPEOzG3VMnf7rKjfNdbrN3gwW3yxWiFD3FQ8ih4IavJRr189fDN3Yr7uMaS2aX4RXUhCXMrOyrxSCjspn-DynmHQC6OlYH74p7yaSDO)\n[2] [ucalgary.ca](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFtM6EcSn0klLVUGxeYHu6RuBEohQpUwiqTdGRWk-iRCB6gHK0VfsUO7_kpPFFnlv1tF0n7ZXsHsiZf1IBfLJOrzRxDkDy5Ex_jlQpt84xT3XXrU6breH5-QpSZJPno66SumyVgQC2TnEQsdk3HQzmp)\n[3] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEJ31_s8jFGIqtEziByAnMHgsATKDD2YSHziGyCmbbVmoWY-iKWU-xCsyEQiqVDM43t8mpY_9Q9VvuXVZwkftaw4rJvfQeckwu7c772PY1tZh8ZV-6De_Au88g4biHf-x37jsp6bps7hqYHRM3XmHQGXj5QmkI96mzeKASi)\n[4] [ucalgary.ca](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFK9TZB0sDGGZLkW3Q6NGQXSEEtQHBkdADJVnvHVRgfHnq0BdbADCzIFeoEpF2_qUd4BZJVOkeQG7AqB3_VLUv9hN60g1XHJiekyPmJlwLc8-QC53x221Qq-J9uY56hdA==)\n[5] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEJ4xkSpja6ymN8kP9-hOAcWfs1u