{"step_index":64,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:46Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:57:46+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:57:48+02:00\nThe search for \"\"DeLaVina\" \"total domination\" \"radius\" OR \"rad(G)\"\" returned the following summary:\nErmelinda DeLaViña and her colleagues have conducted significant research on the **total domination number** ($\\gamma_t(G)$) of graphs, specifically establishing bounds related to the **radius** ($rad(G)$) of a graph[1][2].\n\n### Key Findings\n*   **Lower Bound Theorem:** DeLaViña et al. proved that for any connected graph $G$ with at least two vertices, the total domination number is at least the radius of the graph[1][2]:\n    $$\\gamma_t(G) \\geq rad(G)$$\n*   **Case of Equality:** Further research by the group provides a characterization for when this bound is met[1][2]. Specifically, for a connected graph $G$ and a total dominating set $S$ (where $|S| = \\gamma_t(G)$), the equality $\\gamma_t(G) = rad(G)$ holds if and only if the size of the subgraph induced by $S$, denoted $G[S]$, is $rad(G)/2$[1][2].\n*   **Sharpness:** This bound is known to be sharp; for example, in the case of a path $P_n$ where $n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 4$, both the radius and the total domination number equal $n/2$[1][2].\n\n### Research Context\nThese results are often cited in the context of graph theory research concerning domination parameters[1][2]. The work by DeLaViña, Craig Larson, Ryan Pepper, and Bill Waller frequently appears in literature related to extremal graph theory, particularly in studies involving trees and various domination invariants[3][4][5].\n\nSources:\n[1] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGDam-4E7Kn06yS4GJ8EMwN1GRawn9NJz-ltvauWCjKslZqd6zCD2PdS6CKYQfr7NyHnYS0ysyVkVg5r0XVm_kWWYka4_nMNNt2tcf06Mr8BF2dq7aIancB0WFFyiHhTn-oyn9BM6vNc8bYCn61IggmqRLL-q-31zd0yg==)\n[2] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/groundi