{"step_index":66,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:57:51+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:57:55+02:00\nThe search for \"\"DeLaViña\" \"Larson\" \"Pepper\" \"Waller\" \"total domination\" \"radius\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe terms \"DeLaViña,\" \"Larson,\" \"Pepper,\" and \"Waller\" refer to a group of mathematicians—**Ermelinda DeLaViña, Craig E. Larson, Ryan Pepper, and Bill Waller**—who have co-authored several papers in graph theory[1][2][3].\n\nTheir research often involves the use of the computer program **Graffiti** (or **Graffiti.pc**) to generate and investigate conjectures in graph theory[1][3][4]. Regarding your specific keywords:\n\n*   **Total Domination Number and Radius:** This research group has published work establishing various bounds for graph parameters[3][5]. Notably, it has been shown in their work (and cited in their papers) that the **total domination number** ($\\gamma_t(G)$) of a simple connected graph is at least the **radius** ($r(G)$) of the graph[6][7][8].\n*   **Research Context:** Much of their collaboration focuses on conjectures generated by Graffiti.pc, including bounds related to domination, total domination, independence numbers, and radius[3][9][4]. For example, they have published:\n    *   \"Some conjectures of Graffiti.pc on total domination\" (*Congressus Numerantium*, 2007)[2][9].\n    *   \"On total domination and support vertices of a tree\" (*AKCE International Journal of Graphs and Combinatorics*, 2010)[3][5].\n    *   \"Graffiti.pc on the 2-domination number of a graph\" (*Congressus Numerantium*, 2010)[10][11].\n    *   \"A Characterization of Graphs Where the Independence Number Equals the Radius\" (*Graphs and Combinatorics*, 2012)[12][13].\n\nThe phrase \"total domination\" appears frequently in their work as they analyze lower and upper bounds for this parameter in relation to other graph invariants like radius,