{"step_index":68,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:57Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:57:57+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:01+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Some conjectures of Graffiti.pc on total domination\" \"radius\"\" returned the following summary:\n\"Some conjectures of Graffiti.pc on total domination\" is a research paper published in **Congressus Numerantium (Volume 185, 2007, pp. 81–95)** by **Ermelinda DeLaViña, Qi Liu, Ryan Pepper, Bill Waller, and Douglas B. West**[1][2][3].\n\n### Overview\nThe paper presents and investigates several lower bounds on the **total domination number** of a graph that originated as conjectures from **Graffiti.pc**, a computer program developed by Ermelinda DeLaViña for automated conjecture generation in graph theory[4][5]. The research provides mathematical proofs for many of these conjectures, which often relate the total domination number to other graph invariants such as:\n*   **Radius ($r(G)$):** One of the paper's key findings is that the total domination number of a graph is at least its radius[6].\n*   **Degree and Connectivity:** The work explores how the total domination number relates to parameters like maximum degree and the number of cut-vertices[7].\n*   **Girth ($g(G)$):** The paper discusses specific conjectures, such as one suggesting that for a connected graph $G$ that is not a tree, the total domination number $t(G) \\geq (g(G)+1)/\\delta'(G)$ (where $\\delta'(G)$ is the minimum degree)[8].\n\n### Context in Graph Theory\n*   **Automated Conjecturing:** The paper is part of a broader body of work in the field of automated discovery, where software like Graffiti and its successor, Graffiti.pc, are used to identify non-trivial inequalities between graph invariants[9][5].\n*   **Related Work:** The same research group has published extensively on related invariants, including the standard domination number, independence number, and their relationships with the radius of a graph[2][10][11]. For instance, a related paper by some of the same authors in *Discussiones Mathematicae Graph Theory* (2010) similarly examines lower bounds for the standard domination number, noting that it is at least two-thirds of the radius[2][12].\n\nThe paper serves as a significant reference in the study of total domination and remains frequently cited in research concerning automated conjecturing and distance-based graph invariants[13][10].\n\nSources:\n[1] [google.lu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE7Ud4tb9oxdnHC94hut-a3gucieagZy-XymO0XFZiP4P9BB9CnIP-feRTqjBbnbNDuQxP7nDE7hiJN3ajWfNE33K4TfJxeu5iRkfeMI3k580OzsaND7t6ofGkaaGY9AtGK6pA7uq961jfqvasqwPzOew==)\n[2] [uz.zgora.pl](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF_kBnR30Vhc-ZcfN0_c5lQxHfo21LUV-PpYWb-6QH1WohKjri5iOlLgKArXbsfGD0AYyBvj4x9awwgcco_Ul78CZ-58YIcbVjc8bxxBt_iEcuEKOVdGi0nn_ClLWig-DqCyei7HTwgXNf9Ubay7Kk=)\n[3] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFm1v2P2bGETmtyS1zawBx71TsWZ8bEdkGX-PdqazVC_oSGkBSI3qmEQvRakY8XD5Zwpnyx4BGG81JP9H0lBRnF4sx2cyesMaE1368DOw7rJN3Pf1ir_CjU)\n[4] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFY2dsBa_k64Gj_XWfwu3rEzpQkUKm_2aTrvCoJJM_hmfhDuPQnLyQyUOohhZnGJVc3hpEqgohVjemdTBKn2wCwo3SA3nYB5L8VR3FkzijXl3-NvUc-j4m8SRECy7BXkZvgVcKg-aB56TReOb0lQn0v67rJpQ1-Ic0p9LsW9_QE986Widj6kasDQR1YyzAcGHhfmB1I8ss9Qxa0-Q==)\n[5] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFKDAO0k1fmptzYi6PUMiUBuGU_gSuu2mA3PJVe47rnsWAmpZemuiVbJpHaD5TlRl_lJ_58-kpuqP5IBEXcWknJIkV6fUfh4QVTBU8sUsgbOT21ADwPF3TYpXicy7KtLiqkhWSuSLjrsXzpmdX7VMgA47XmW5na_mdx3mRvi4d-WA==)\n[6] [icm.edu.pl](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFmWPYK6N_vCv4XYuD4PiLHZjcr_IPYpJXMMdQTKcd69O8F9OGmUmnkF8FamncXfmneJbNIiv06Q0MJJWzH9v5jGHoJzA8nwOdJ2gAa_4NDqgK0qdJq0zWGhrrOuM6XoCwsPG8pDD8EUFmn2ADWC9Nm90rQr8PhvFuYER