{"step_index":72,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:07Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:07+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:11+02:00\nThe search for \"site:math.illinois.edu/~dwest \"total domination\" \"radius\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe research and conjectures regarding \"total domination\" and \"radius\" hosted on Douglas West's website primarily relate to his work with the conjecturing program **Graffiti.pc**[1] and his collaborative research on graph theory[2][3].\n\n### Key References on Douglas West's Website\n*   **Graffiti.pc Conjectures:** Douglas West's page on \"Some Conjectures of Graffiti.pc (2004-07)\" lists several conjectures relating the total domination number $\\gamma_t(G)$ to the radius $r(G)$ and the minimum degree $\\delta(G)$[1]. For example:\n    *   One conjecture suggests that if $G$ is connected, then $\\gamma_t(G) \\geq 2r(G)/\\delta(G)$[1].\n*   **Corrections:** His \"Introduction to Graph Theory - corrections\" page includes references to exercise clarifications involving the term \"radius\"[4].\n*   **Collaborative Research:** West is a co-author of papers—such as \"Some Conjectures of Graffiti.pc on Total Domination\"—that explore these relationships as part of a broader study of domination parameters[2][3].\n\n### Related Mathematical Context\nIn the broader graph theory literature, the relationship between the total domination number $\\gamma_t(G)$ and the radius $r(G)$ is a well-studied topic[5][6][7]:\n*   **Lower Bounds:** It is known that for a connected graph $G$ of order at least two, the radius provides a lower bound for the total domination number (often cited as $\\gamma_t(G) \\geq \\text{rad}(G)$ in specific contexts or classes of graphs)[8][9].\n*   **Total Domination Polynomials:** Research also exists on the roots of total domination polynomials, where the radius of the circle containing these roots is defined in terms of the graph's order $n$ and minimum degree $\\delta$[10][11].\n\nSources:\n[1] [illinois.edu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEk2QN70YpNsYODBFpaBXfSKukiP7TcHPP3ldRyGu_FmbClwMSIGcDX7IJBO3dln-pnu3qC8rVQlst-rVaIt8LIDmd92ABr9b0_iZh6bqUkHcm6eszhYqB3EuFG1VPX-VDH-KsqxDIV)\n[2] [georgiasouthern.edu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG6_ERqBHmJzqVf18FdcMFz1akMfyQ-DhYG6m8vRmv5mXitQD3dZ52ORdvDJw-KgrLhPusCcXor1n_LbgYsreOGbk9w0BMkl1V_kcegFiy9tDV7h2kNkD57SufP6wcgZtJX0h5PeKutISSHwrP84YQM60-pfe-pUyjrymY-JMQWfMuQ6Cjm28quukDFP9o=)\n[3] [eudml.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHS9t2wC0Bh-4j3NLVMyYnt26TmLPn34C-qKXDKSsxSP_QONgifYWcq-Rs6SxpbaVF9-Ki7iJMrrzK4KKCevdLViyNdcD0DzGZ5_3eQEKKdfb9o)\n[4] [illinois.edu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFLW0BjBPL2s2r6-I3YWdAk4rdTmpwsf3x6dQxsp-wT18tb3bs7kfOh9yUP1DUg15SfilwT-vvSzsjUMl-gubcOq6t6bcECxETPGsZYgvJbnGFVZsw7JpM8P-WF8aQJ2-bPcna08A==)\n[5] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGtrhUSvCyt6Y3qQfVYDhgg7_R_aTDPNhVqnkau7UhmLBMFTd-vBCaDxRVqMo_0FkcmOBfxSvOZr15Ru66-XR71sGAIqLayZtKQEZHV689NgDnQae8EMLWhMkc2g3NDsez2OszB6cYQGuqubM0m-ATMQ2tYFx-BH-qTrOVciybWcS4BFyW6L-xiVHcdgOdSOwcJiFf9mASF641nTvXoTjQFsd9kwFAKO1aouyp79hmS2NDtEIhcEf0zXz2xz4Y2j0WmFQ==)\n[6] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF3iBZXrMTJx3QAJQia0NwamQJNeu9_Ky069HXHsqyVh2Dd_tYSRBuPh44zgOrQlCI0zobn9xB8atR6ZkRwm6tMW2qW1MsfaeT2oxGgBVWcGJX4r_g8_nFyVxUlFvzf3wh1IOY=)\n[7] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHNSp93s28bj3Hyu1yUUTVlVcavkDl7MgGmrHoU5Cgfry2cyh691ZRmEL_x5Y286APjBo_KIAWGXuEpCOPT9yfNCA4LPxXeoTRH9Ria7NqeZiKDpRSSmGuqjWRa253904e18U5igfw-nHnVAVViJnohNUwSGD9RDl-VmelKrQ==)\n[8] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH95WFldBgYvcRvBLvDSatocLGqnyMCz8un-9miHuBMy5v8AoFhCBjmLx-_TfPHWU0WX_zsb2NggtVO6tfIc