{"step_index":76,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:06Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:59:06+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:59:09+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Henning\" \"Yeo\" \"Total Domination in Graphs\" \"rad(G)\" OR \"radius\" \"proof\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn the monograph *Total Domination in Graphs* (2013) by Michael A. Henning and Anders Yeo, the authors provide several fundamental bounds relating the total domination number, $\\gamma_t(G)$, to other graph invariants, including the radius of a graph, $\\text{rad}(G)$[1][2].\n\nSpecifically, they state the following result:\n\n**Proposition:** If $G$ is a connected graph of order at least two, then $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)$[1].\n\n### Context and Related Bounds\nThe book serves as a primary reference for total domination theory[3][2]. In addition to the inequality involving the radius, the text includes several related lower bounds for the total domination number:\n\n*   **Diameter:** For a connected graph $G$ of order at least two, $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\frac{\\text{diam}(G) + 1}{2}$[1].\n*   **Girth:** For a graph with girth $g$, $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge g/2$[1].\n*   **Domination Number:** For a graph with no isolated vertices, $\\gamma(G) \\le \\gamma_t(G) \\le 2\\gamma(G)$[1][4].\n\nThe proofs for these and other results are contained within the technical sections of the monograph, which covers both algorithmic aspects and structural graph theory related to total domination[5][2]. For the formal proof of the radius bound, readers are typically referred to the specific chapter addressing lower bounds in the *Total Domination in Graphs* text[1][2].\n\nSources:\n[1] [jomardpublishing.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG8l9G4ovd4jM9AwTGChCPa6yk6C6JvfTaA1t_ZWkijOPINBjjWunShT66sttTJG7ajCMyJf_dkJrXyqVCHdwRtrGnhKJRmUjgBBUeKzMQaztYbN9e-Lh_9qm8Xfdk-xF9LItqZ2A0-_maIBpJO-EuKG2vH8th-QOQkaFyA8tD56-hUt_eckQ==)\