{"step_index":78,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:12Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:59:12+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:59:16+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Total Domination in Graphs\" \"rad(G)\" \"Proposition\" OR \"Theorem\" OR \"Proof\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the relationship between the **total domination number** $\\gamma_t(G)$ and the **radius** $\\text{rad}(G)$ of a connected graph $G$ is a well-studied topic. Several results establish lower bounds for the total domination number in terms of the radius and diameter of the graph.\n\n### Key Results and Theorems\n\n*   **General Lower Bound:** It is a known result in the study of total domination that for any connected graph $G$ of order $n \\ge 2$ (with no isolated vertices), the total domination number is bounded below by the radius[1][2]:\n    $$\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)$$\n*   **Total Domination Integrity:** Research into \"Total Domination Integrity\" (TDI)—a parameter related to the total domination number—also utilizes the radius in its bounds[3][4]. Specifically:\n    *   **Theorem:** For a connected graph $G$ of order $n > 2$ with no isolated vertices, $\\text{TDI}(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G) + 1$[3][4].\n*   **Distance $k$-Domination:** The bound $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)$ has been generalized to the total distance $k$-domination number, $\\gamma_{t,k}(G)$[1]. For $k \\ge 1$:\n    $$\\gamma_{t,k}(G) \\ge \\frac{1}{k} \\text{rad}(G)$$\n    This is derived by noting that a spanning tree $T$ exists such that $\\gamma_{t,k}(G) = \\gamma_{t,k}(T)$ and that $\\text{rad}(G) \\le \\text{rad}(T)$[1].\n\n### Contextual Notes\n*   **Total Domination Number ($\\gamma_t(G)$):** Defined as the minimum cardinality of a set $S \\subseteq V(G)$ such that every vertex in $V(G)$ is adjacent to at least one vertex in $S$ (requiring no isolated vertices)[5][6][7].\n*   **Radius ($\\text{rad}(G)$):** Defined as the minimum eccentricity among all vertices in $G$, where the eccentricity of a vertex is the maximum distance to any other vertex in the graph[8][9][10].\n*   **Applications:** These bounds are frequently used in structural graph theory to analyze how the \"spread\" of a graph (its radius or diameter) forces a minimum number of vertices to be included in a dominating set[1][11].\n\nSources:\n[1] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGFtIE8TY_Fb5Xcrx_TPeD9bWm19OwOOgalQGvtYGw1wtjnIB46u8pq9xSxjcU0OWfYqiyFDcSAHZc_ftqVHhJbFXMQSF-IM6jDEVy5e9-ap805hhRimw==)\n[2] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGDruRSPveJh_CGZXlSfEa-Wnst8Mz7UFHjgtNNT5rtGlIq2_4Q0TqqVPPUC-ihZjFXeJFfk_R1LPrL6z8KN5SK1ANwSK6VcgPX8Cp0EVgiLMZkLEp7_pD44oZ67E8HiL8Scyz6fqW5LKnSgNLJocWqV4qJXkyLQZNNpQ==)\n[3] [jomardpublishing.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHgURYtgOgaYEz2Wfz3s_3Owm0ZFUqD05ALtfMfZJSb-u7onMIut7L_Oz9IEw3LqBpFQSZQfsmZr9X9IbAqzDuA4ywjdgSIfcT8nyhek63awI2exSc_CTwrIl4i8le5rJqPqTEAr7j-GyQbt8PpfvA9stGlKglTNvQt41s3NjLk9R73yd9rNDg=)\n[4] [jomardpublishing.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHgURYtgOgaYEz2Wfz3s_3Owm0ZFUqD05ALtfMfZJSb-u7onMIut7L_Oz9IEw3LqBpFQSZQfsmZr9X9IbAqzDuA4ywjdgSIfcT8nyhek63awI2exSc_CTwrIl4i8le5rJqPqTEAr7j-GyQbt8PpfvA9stGlKglTNvQt41s3NjLk9R73yd9rNDg=)\n[5] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG3xwc3X-6cOJEoeKR8ldbxONu8pfmnAtt4uJkkgc1J2pB6O-yBrXXa07XWZAyLDi-wHDauNhLA1Zfsm4vPqMR06Uo0ORCP2H8SJ1LmR7SzC7T2NiOQ14_jXNCvJ-ouqdigtBSPLm5V3KVp5B3CAByDtDBhI35gpnwxiVLibOtVjbb2Sw84vC9xjQ==)\n[6] [uni-ulm.de](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG5bxPlBU43jIpX0yPa87dBiW6-HESAj4gF93k16yLNShfkSR5JmvTJF9T2C3zKFA3vPv0MS1lR62IJ0OuOokeXIeTKH8yY-SVqsTOXmeO7UQ-Eu9E2GZQ7rrHYrzenjGhucuWTK6xLEDSH-y-l8RP1ojcMMRh1VAUp_Mdu_cgFz39vbbfe9sKipsfyvzOXimYjDY3nwYU8EHZsLhMXVEGE-ZQ8Uw_4yrTLqsI19